 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534162 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534163 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534167 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534168 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534173 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1039 "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186534177 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186092119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092263 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1045 "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186092399 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9ed557a5-e06f-5f5a-9230-32a93a80b727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092618 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092618 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092618 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092618 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e52fa16-cf3e-5339-95e2-786fe7ef7e5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092618 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093116 #edb/ref 1061 646582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87b44f73-3af6-53c8-a8c8-61525491b5d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093117 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093117 #edb/ref 1061 646583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093117 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a179fdba-d910-50b0-81ea-369c06993adf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093118 #edb/ref 1061 646589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093118 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8a87a08-8a33-5f7d-a2ee-3bb4f46035fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093118 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093120 #edb/ref 1061 646586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e3bd15e6-53e0-51f6-bb04-9b4ea73b76c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093369 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate exponential functions like \n\n$$\ny = e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u = 3x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=3x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093369 #edb/ref 1065 1268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093369 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cf67b8fb-2658-5221-b309-6cf09e93b5e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093369 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285123 #edb/ref 1080 "x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285123 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285123 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "265b541b-62a2-540d-bd79-5ef733742b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285478 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2{x}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285478 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285478 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a81ce5d-011d-51ee-bf9c-6333bf4dc66f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285492 #edb/ref 1080 "2{e}^{{x}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285492 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285492 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b97230b-e5a8-5328-9497-45595399fb66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285975 #edb/ref 1080 "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285975 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285975 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "812af0ad-7d34-5c50-a85a-78aa2431dcbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286818 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286818 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286818 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dedd5724-68fc-54e4-9b62-8ebbbd324624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186286818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186285975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287169 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0cfdfe24-c411-57d2-aa42-0fbbe96ad9c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534162 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534166 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534162 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534162 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1}$ at the point where $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534162 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07e0afd0-4729-561c-85c0-8bd11d965ce5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534162 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}+6x) \\\\ & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 x^{2} + 6 x\\right) e^{- 2 x^{3} + 3 x^{2} - 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(-6(2)^{2}+6(2))e^{-2(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1} \\\\ & =-12\\cdot e^{-5} \\\\ & =- \\frac{12}{e^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534163 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534176 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534163 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534163 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1}$ at the point where $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0be68eb4-fee3-576f-ac3a-d663b1b8b84c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534163 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\ & =(3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(3 x^{2} - 4 x\\right) e^{x^{3} - 2 x^{2} - 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(3(2)^{2}-4(2))e^{(2)^{3}-2(2)^{2}-1} \\\\ & =4\\cdot e^{-1} \\\\ & =\\frac{4}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534167 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534167 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534167 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1}$ at the point where $x=-2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534167 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cb5cb4c-646e-54f0-9994-bb70583c546b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534167 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\ & =(-3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 x^{2} - 4 x\\right) e^{- x^{3} - 2 x^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=-2} & =(-3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1}|_{x=-2} \\\\ & =(-3(-2)^{2}-4(-2))e^{-(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\ & =-4\\cdot e^{1} \\\\ & =- 4 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534164 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534168 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+4}$ at the point where $x=-2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50ee9513-e646-50a4-ac90-7dd2ba8b0b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534168 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{x}^{3}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-4{x}^{3}+6x) \\\\ & =(-4{x}^{3}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 x^{3} + 6 x\\right) e^{- x^{4} + 3 x^{2} + 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=-2} & =(-4{x}^{3}+6x)e^{-{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+4}|_{x=-2} \\\\ & =(-4(-2)^{3}+6(-2))e^{-(-2)^{4}+3(-2)^{2}+4} \\\\ & =20\\cdot e^{0} \\\\ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534170 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534173 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4}$ at the point where $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9053d53-838d-5e57-a71a-28c1ae22fb94" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534173 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}+6x) \\\\ & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 x^{2} + 6 x\\right) e^{- 2 x^{3} + 3 x^{2} - 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(-6(2)^{2}+6(2))e^{-2(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\ & =-12\\cdot e^{-8} \\\\ & =- \\frac{12}{e^{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534177 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186092263.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186534177 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "c2d7a76c-5607-5777-aed3-5f05373fc7bb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546070 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546025 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=-4{e}^{-5-x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05c06d18-760a-55d8-8f2d-5ce452eb9a43" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546025 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-4{e}^{u},\\,u=-5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{-5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546024 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546033 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{5}-{e}^{4-5x}.$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b4c3b4f-ee6c-5156-ab65-2225d5039897" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546033 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y={e}^{4-5x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4-5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{4-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}-{e}^{4-5x}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}-(-5{e}^{4-5x}) \\\\  & =5({x}^{4}+{e}^{4-5x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546023 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546037 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=2x+{e}^{4x-6}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46c70445-c7fa-5e96-b9db-24423c65d9d2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546037 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y={e}^{4x-6}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+{e}^{4x-6}) \\\\  & =2+4{e}^{4x-6} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x-6}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546039 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546076 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546039 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546039 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=3{e}^{-4x-6}+6$ at the point where $x=- \\frac{7}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546039 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52a84e58-ee14-59cb-b2fc-5dc806f58a59" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546039 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{u}+6,\\,u=-4x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-4x-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=- \\frac{7}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} & =-12{e}^{-4x-6}{|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ & =-12{e}^{(-4)(- \\frac{7}{4})-6} \\\\ & =- 12 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=- \\frac{7}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(3{e}^{-4x-6}+6){|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ & =3{e}^{(-4)(- \\frac{7}{4})-6}+6 \\\\ & =6 + 3 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6 + 3 e) & =(- 12 e)(x-(- \\frac{7}{4})) \\\\ y-(6 + 3 e) & =- 12 e x-(21 e) \\\\ y & =- 12 e x - 18 e + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546056 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546047 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-2{e}^{-3x+2}+3$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fd12a30-30af-5a77-a4c8-bf313d7546d2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546047 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=-3x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6{e}^{u} \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\frac{1}{3}} & =6{e}^{-3x+2}{|}_{x=\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ & =6{e}^{(-3)(\\frac{1}{3})+2} \\\\ & =6 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=\\frac{1}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(-2{e}^{-3x+2}+3){|}_{x=\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ & =-2{e}^{(-3)(\\frac{1}{3})+2}+3 \\\\ & =3 - 2 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(3 - 2 e) & =(6 e)(x-(\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\ y-(3 - 2 e) & =6 e x-(2 e) \\\\ y & =6 e x - 4 e + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546030 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546052 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x) = e^{-4x}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f144a62-dd63-5191-a104-7945db3746f9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546052 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546036 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546058 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{e}^{6x-4}+1$ at the point where $x=\\frac{5}{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b32ededd-4f82-5701-b090-5a6b3fd40512" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546058 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u}+1,\\,u=6x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =30{e}^{u} \\\\  & =30{e}^{6x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{5}{6}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} & =30{e}^{6x-4}{|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ & =30{e}^{(6)(\\frac{5}{6})-4} \\\\ & =30 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=\\frac{5}{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(5{e}^{6x-4}+1){|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ & =5{e}^{(6)(\\frac{5}{6})-4}+1 \\\\ & =1 + 5 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1 + 5 e) & =(30 e)(x-(\\frac{5}{6})) \\\\ y-(1 + 5 e) & =30 e x-(25 e) \\\\ y & =30 e x - 20 e + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546061 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546062 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-4{e}^{3x-5}-3$ at the point where $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6bcf79b-eac1-52f9-ad10-1945c83effb0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546062 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-4{e}^{u}-3,\\,u=3x-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-12{e}^{3x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=2} & =-12{e}^{3x-5}{|}_{x=2} \\\\ & =-12{e}^{(3)(2)-5} \\\\ & =- 12 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(-4{e}^{3x-5}-3){|}_{x=2} \\\\ & =-4{e}^{(3)(2)-5}-3 \\\\ & =- 4 e - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(- 4 e - 3) & =(- 12 e)(x-(2)) \\\\ y-(- 4 e - 3) & =- 12 e x-(- 24 e) \\\\ y & =- 12 e x - 3 + 20 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546071 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546066 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-2{e}^{2x-5}-3$ at the point where $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d323abf4-feef-5783-8c9c-89b3cff14f40" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186546066 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{u}-3,\\,u=2x-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{2x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$, we have to evaluate $\\fra